{
  "id": "edwardian_coastal_fort",
  "title": "Edwardian Coastal Headland Fort",
  "description": "A late-thirteenth-century Edwardian Welsh stone fortress on a coastal headland: a rectangular curtain enclosure with corner drum towers, a twin-tower gatehouse, arrow loops, a tall keep, and a domestic courtyard with great hall, chapel and kitchen ranges, protected by an approach ditch.",
  "style_id": "edwardian_welsh",
  "site_id": "coastal_headland",
  "prompt": "You are a master mason serving Edward I in Wales. On the coastal headland, enclose a bailey with a battered stone curtain and four round corner drum towers. Place a twin-tower gatehouse in the south curtain, pierce the curtains with splayed arrow loops, and raise a tall square keep with the lord's apartments. Around the courtyard add a great hall, a chapel range and a kitchen range under pitched roofs. Cut a wide approach ditch across the landward side. Keep every wall thick enough and battered, and leave the gate passage clear.",
